  "title": "When velocity is hard to measure: evaluating engineering…",
  "content": "The conventional advice on engineering managers is incomplete in one specific, costly way. Here's the fuller picture, with the tradeoffs left in. The answers online were either wrong or skipped the part that mattered.\n\n## The symptom: “I can’t tell if my team is actually delivering”\n\nEvery engineering manager eventually hits a review cycle where the usual signals fail. The sprint burndown looks fine, but the roadmap hasn’t moved. Story points are up, but the same incidents keep recurring. Or the opposite: the team looks quiet on the board, yet customers are reporting fewer bugs and the on-call rotation is calmer. When you can’t point to a number that reliably reflects output, you’re left making judgment calls that feel arbitrary — and your team can tell.\n\nThe real problem isn’t that you lack a metric. It’s that you’re trying to use a single metric for a system that has multiple, loosely coupled outputs. The fix is to build a small portfolio of evidence that triangulates output without pretending to be precise. This post gives you a decision framework, a worked example, and a checklist you can apply in your next one-on-one.\n\n## Why velocity breaks as a measure of output\n\nVelocity — completed story points per sprint — was never designed to measure value. It was designed to help a team forecast its own capacity. The moment it becomes a management target, three things happen:\n\n1. **Point inflation.** If points are tied to performance reviews, the cheapest way to improve the number is to estimate higher. This isn’t malicious; it’s rational. The metric stops measuring work and starts measuring negotiation skill.\n2. **Slicing distortion.** Large, valuable items get broken into many small items to inflate counts, while genuinely complex work gets avoided because it doesn’t fit neatly into a sprint.\n3. **Local optimization.** Teams optimize for “done” in the board sense — tickets closed — rather than “done” in the operational sense — deployed, observed, and stable.\n\nNone of this means velocity is useless. It means velocity is a *capacity signal*, not an *output signal*. Using it as an output signal is a category error, like using CPU utilization to measure whether a service is healthy. High CPU can mean load or it can mean a busy loop.\n\nThe same logic applies to lines of code, number of commits, or tickets closed. Each is a proxy that can be gamed or can drift from what actually matters. The question isn’t “which metric is best?” but “what portfolio of evidence is hard to game and cheap to collect?”\n\n## A decision framework: three layers of evidence\n\nInstead of one number, evaluate output across three layers, each answering a different question:\n\n| Layer | Question it answers | Example signals | Gaming risk | Collection cost |\n|-------|---------------------|-----------------|-------------|-----------------|\n| **Delivery** | Are we shipping what we said we would? | Roadmap items completed, cycle time from “in progress” to “deployed,” deployment frequency | Medium — can be gamed by shrinking scope | Low — already in your tracker and CI |\n| **Stability** | Is what we shipped staying shipped? | Change failure rate, mean time to restore (MTTR), incident count by severity, error budget burn | Low — incidents are hard to hide | Medium — requires incident tracking and monitoring |\n| **Outcome** | Is the work moving a business or user metric? | Adoption of the feature, support ticket volume for the area, conversion or retention for the surface | High — attribution is fuzzy | High — requires instrumentation and agreement on the metric |\n\nUse all three. Delivery alone rewards shipping junk. Stability alone rewards doing nothing. Outcome alone is too noisy at short intervals and too easy to attribute wrongly. Together, they form a triangle: if any two sides look good and the third doesn’t, you have a specific conversation to have.\n\nThis is not original to me — it’s a restatement of the DORA metrics (deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, time to restore) combined with product outcome thinking. The value is in using them as a *portfolio*, not as a scorecard.\n\n## Worked example: two teams, same velocity, different output\n\nAssume two teams, both with a velocity of 40 points per sprint (illustrative numbers, not measured). Team A ships 8 small items per sprint; Team B ships 3 larger items. On velocity alone, they look identical. Now add the other layers:\n\n- **Team A:** change failure rate 25% (2 of 8 deploys require a hotfix), MTTR 4 hours, one Sev-2 incident per sprint. Outcome: feature adoption flat because the feature keeps breaking.\n- **Team B:** change failure rate 8% (roughly 1 in 12 deploys), MTTR 45 minutes, no Sev-2 in the last three sprints. Outcome: adoption up 10% quarter-over-quarter (assume the metric is instrumented and agreed).\n\nSame velocity, very different output. The conversation with Team A isn’t “work harder” — it’s “your change failure rate is eating your delivery capacity; let’s look at test coverage and deploy size.” The conversation with Team B isn’t “great job” — it’s “can you document what you’re doing so we can spread it?”\n\nThe point of the example is not the specific numbers. It’s that velocity alone would have hidden the difference. The three-layer view makes the difference legible without requiring a perfect metric.\n\n## Fix 1 — Replace “velocity” with a delivery-and-stability pair in your reviews\n\nThe most common cause of “I can’t measure output” is that the review agenda only has one slot: velocity. Change the agenda. For each team, prepare two numbers before the review: **deployment frequency** and **change failure rate**. Both are computable from data you already have if you use a CI/CD system and an issue tracker.\n\n- Deployment frequency: count of production deploys in the period, divided by the number of working days. If you deploy manually, count releases.\n- Change failure rate: number of deploys that required a hotfix or rollback within a defined window (e.g., 24 hours), divided by total deploys.\n\nYou don’t need a dashboard to start. A spreadsheet with two columns per team, updated weekly, is enough to have the conversation. The act of collecting the number changes what people pay attention to.\n\n**Symptom this fixes:** reviews where the only question is “why is velocity down?” and the answer is always “estimates changed.”\n\n## Fix 2 — Instrument the outcome, even crudely\n\nThe less obvious cause is that outcome data exists but isn’t connected to engineering work. A feature ships, and nobody checks whether support tickets for that area went down. The fix is to pick one outcome metric per major initiative and define it *before* the work starts.\n\nA practical pattern: for each roadmap item, write a one-line “success statement” in the ticket — e.g., “Reduce password-reset tickets by 20% within 30 days of launch.” It doesn’t have to be perfectly measurable; it has to be specific enough that someone can check it. At the next review, ask “did we check?” If the answer is no, that’s the finding — not a failure of the team, but a gap in the process.\n\nThis is where GDPR and data-residency constraints matter. If your outcome metric involves user data, you need a lawful basis for processing it and a retention policy. A crude but compliant approach is to use aggregated, anonymized counts (e.g., number of reset requests per day) rather than per-user tracking. If you’re in the EU, check whether the metric requires consent under your jurisdiction’s implementation of the ePrivacy Directive; often aggregate operational metrics do not, but per-user behavioral tracking does. Document the basis in the ticket. This isn’t bureaucracy — it’s what makes the metric defensible if someone asks.\n\n**Symptom this fixes:** “We shipped it, but we don’t know if it helped.”\n\n## Fix 3 — Use incident and on-call data as a stability signal\n\nThe environment-specific cause is that stability data lives in a different tool (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, incident.io) from delivery data (Jira, Linear), and nobody joins them. Join them monthly.\n\nA minimal join: for each incident, record the service or feature area and the deploy that preceded it (if any). Then compute, per team, the number of incidents per deploy and the MTTR. You don’t need a data warehouse; a shared spreadsheet with three columns (date, service, linked deploy) is enough to see patterns.\n\nWhat you’re looking for is not “zero incidents” — that’s a sign of under-deployment or hidden failures. You’re looking for a *stable or declining* incident rate as deployment frequency increases. If deployment frequency goes up and incident rate goes up proportionally, you’re shipping faster but not safer; the next investment is in testing and observability, not more features.\n\n**Symptom this fixes:** “The team says they’re busy, but the same things keep breaking.”\n\n## How to verify the new approach is working\n\nYou can’t prove causality from a few sprints, but you can check for three leading indicators:\n\n1. **The review conversation changes.** Instead of “why is velocity down?”, the questions become “what’s our change failure rate?” and “did we check the outcome metric?” If the conversation doesn’t change, the data isn’t being used.\n2. **Teams can explain their own numbers.** Ask a team lead to walk through their deployment frequency and change failure rate without preparation. If they can’t, the data isn’t visible enough.\n3. **At least one decision changes.** A roadmap item gets re-scoped because the outcome metric was unrealistic, or a testing investment gets prioritized because change failure rate is high. If nothing changes, you’ve added reporting without adding value.\n\nA simple verification script can pull deployment counts from your CI system’s API (e.g., GitHub Actions runs or GitLab pipelines) and incident counts from your incident tool, then print a weekly summary. Here’s a minimal Python example that assumes you export two CSVs — one of deploys with a timestamp, one of incidents with a timestamp and service — and computes the two metrics. It’s illustrative, not production-ready, but it shows the arithmetic.\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# Assume deploys.csv: timestamp,service\n# Assume incidents.csv: timestamp,service,severity\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path) as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\ndeploys = load(\"deploys.csv\")\nincidents = load(\"incidents.csv\")\n\n# Count deploys per service in the last 30 days\ncutoff = datetime.utcnow() - timedelta(days=30)\ndeploy_counts = defaultdict(int)\nfor d in deploys:\n    ts = datetime.fromisoformat(d[\"timestamp\"])\n    if ts >= cutoff:\n        deploy_counts[d[\"service\"]] += 1\n\n# Count Sev-1/Sev-2 incidents per service in the same window\nincident_counts = defaultdict(int)\nfor i in incidents:\n    ts = datetime.fromisoformat(i[\"timestamp\"])\n    if ts >= cutoff and i[\"severity\"] in (\"Sev1\", \"Sev2\"):\n        incident_counts[i[\"service\"]] += 1\n\nfor service in sorted(deploy_counts):\n    d = deploy_counts[service]\n    i = incident_counts.get(service, 0)\n    rate = (i / d) if d else 0.0\n    print(f\"{service}: {d} deploys, {i} incidents, {rate:.2f} incidents/deploy\")\n```\n\nRun it weekly. The absolute numbers matter less than the trend. If incidents per deploy is rising while deploys are flat, something is degrading. If it’s falling, whatever you changed is working — or the sample is too small. Either way, you now have a basis for a conversation that isn’t about story points.\n\n## How to prevent the problem from recurring\n\nMake the portfolio part of the operating rhythm, not a one-off exercise:\n\n- **Quarterly:** review the three layers with each team. Pick one metric per layer to improve next quarter. Don’t try to improve all three at once.\n- **Monthly:** join incident data with deploy data and share the result with the team. Make it visible where they already look (a Slack channel, a dashboard).\n- **Per initiative:** write the outcome statement before work starts and check it after launch. If you can’t check it, say so explicitly and decide whether to instrument it next time.\n- **In one-on-ones:** ask “what’s the most valuable thing you shipped this month, and how do you know it was valuable?” The answer tells you more than any dashboard.\n\nThe anti-pattern to avoid is turning the portfolio into a scorecard with targets. The moment “change failure rate must be below X%” becomes a performance goal, teams will under-report incidents or avoid risky deploys. Use the numbers to ask better questions, not to rank people.\n\n## Related errors and symptoms you might hit next\n\n- **“Our velocity is high but nothing reaches production.”** This is a delivery-layer failure: work is “done” in the tracker but not deployed. Check your definition of done and whether deploys are blocked by manual steps.\n- **“We deploy often but customers keep reporting the same bug.”** Stability-layer failure masked by high deployment frequency. Look at change failure rate and whether hotfixes are counted as deploys.\n- **“We can’t agree on an outcome metric.”** This is usually a sign that the initiative is too vague. Split it into smaller pieces, each with a checkable statement.\n- **“The team feels surveilled.”** This happens when metrics are collected without context and used in reviews without warning. Introduce the portfolio in a team meeting, explain what it is and isn’t for, and let the team see the data before you do.\n\n## When none of these work: escalation path\n\nIf you’ve tried the three layers and still can’t get a clear picture, the problem is likely organizational, not measurement. Two common cases:\n\n1. **The team is dependent on another team.** If most work is blocked on an external dependency, no metric on your team will show output because the bottleneck is elsewhere. Escalate with data: show cycle time broken down by wait state. The conversation is about the dependency, not the team.\n2. **The work is genuinely exploratory.** Research spikes and platform migrations don’t produce shippable features at a steady rate. In that case, measure *learning* instead: number of hypotheses tested, decisions made, or risks retired. Be explicit that this is a different mode and set expectations accordingly.\n\nIf neither applies and you still feel blind, ask for a skip-level review of the team’s work with a peer manager. Sometimes an outside read of the same evidence surfaces a pattern you’ve normalized.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do you measure engineering productivity without velocity?**\nUse a portfolio: delivery (deployment frequency, cycle time), stability (change failure rate, MTTR), and outcome (a specific metric per initiative). No single number is sufficient; the portfolio is designed to be triangulated, not summed. The goal is to ask better questions in reviews, not to produce a definitive score.\n\n**What are good alternatives to story points for output?**\nCount deployed items and their outcomes, not estimated effort. If you need forecasting, keep story points for the team’s internal use but don’t use them as a management output metric. Alternatives like cycle time and throughput measure flow, not value; pair them with stability and outcome data.\n\n**How do you evaluate a team when the work is exploratory?**\nMeasure learning artifacts: hypotheses written, experiments run, decisions documented, risks retired. Set expectations that exploratory work has a different cadence and that “no shippable feature” is not the same as “no output.” Agree on what evidence of progress looks like before the work starts.\n\n**What if leadership demands a single number?**\nOffer a composite only if you also show the components. A single number invites gaming; a composite with visible parts invites conversation. If forced, use deployment frequency as the headline and keep change failure rate as the guardrail — never report one without the other.\n\n## Your next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your issue tracker and export the last 30 days of completed items for one team. Add two columns: “deployed?” (yes/no) and “outcome checked?” (yes/no). Count the no’s. That count is your starting point — it tells you which layer of the portfolio is weakest, and it’s the first thing to fix before your next review.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
